`toyos-fat32`'s host tests read these volumes, and write into the two blank
ones. The only honest judge of a FAT reader is bytes a FAT implementation that
is not ours wrote, and these are those bytes. Each is committed as its runs of
nonzero 512-byte sectors, which `Image::fixture` in
`toyos-fat32/tests/common/mod.rs` expands into a sparse file; the raw digest is
of that expansion, and equals the digest of what the tools wrote.

They were made once, on macOS 27.0.1 (build 26A434), with Apple's `msdosfs`
project at SourceVersion 845000003000001 (`/sbin/newfs_msdos`, sha256
29b3d7daa299583015c823ea6ebce643c030a79c8bc26b9bf9c23deb4c6d1a73) and
`/usr/bin/hdiutil`, by a throwaway program making the invocations
`toyos-fat32/tests/common/mod.rs` made at fa134154c with a fixed label. Per
volume, on a sparse file of the raw size:

    hdiutil attach -imagekey diskimage-class=CRawDiskImage -nobrowse -nomount <file>
    newfs_msdos -F 32 -S 512 -c <c> -v <label> /dev/disk4
    hdiutil detach /dev/disk4
    hdiutil attach -imagekey diskimage-class=CRawDiskImage -nobrowse <file>
    # the files below written under /Volumes/<label> with std::fs, parents
    # created, in order; then every `._*` sidecar macOS left there deleted
    hdiutil detach /dev/disk4

    volume      -c  label        files
    blank-512    1  TFBLANK      none
    blank-4k     8  TFBLANK4K    none
    read-512     1  TFREAD       the ten `populated` lists in tests/host_read.rs
    read-4k      8  TFREAD4K     the three `a_four_kib_cluster_volume_reads_the_same` lists
    hostile      1  TFHOSTILE    sub/deeper/ (mkdir -p first), plain.txt "a short file",
                                 "A Long Name For Entries.bin" pattern(20000, 5),
                                 sub/inner.dat pattern(4000, 6), sub/deeper/leaf.txt "leaf"
    empty-file   1  TFEMPTYFILE  empty.txt "", full.txt "twelve bytes"

`pattern` is the one in `toyos-fat32/tests/common/mod.rs`. Nothing in this
repository runs either tool: the suite reads only what is committed here.

**What in them is not ours.** The names and contents of the files are this
repository's test bytes, and the layout — boot sector, FSInfo, FATs, directory
entries — is the published on-disk format, data the tools computed rather than
a work of theirs. Two things the tools wrote are not:

- Every boot sector, sector 0 and its backup at sector 6, carries at offset
  0x5A the 84 bytes of `newfs_msdos`'s `bootcode[]`: 39 bytes of x86 that print
  a message through the BIOS, wait for a key and reboot, then "Non-system disk"
  and "Press any key to reboot". They are byte for byte that array in FreeBSD's
  `sbin/newfs_msdos/mkfs_msdos.c`, Robert Nordier's under BSD-2-Clause, and in
  Apple's `msdosfs` at tag `msdosfs-788.100.31`
  (`lib_newfs_msdos/newfs_data_types.h`, taken from
  `newfs_msdos.tproj/newfs_msdos.c`, which carries Nordier's notice under
  Apple's). The volumes are declared as carrying it under BSD-2-Clause, whose
  one condition on redistribution in binary form is reproducing the notice,
  which the licence text above does verbatim.
- The freed clusters still hold the AppleDouble sidecars macOS wrote beside
  every file and the generation then deleted: Apple's AppleDouble header with
  its `Mac OS X` filler, an eleven-byte `com.apple.provenance` attribute, and
  the stock phrase "This resource fork intentionally left blank". That is
  machine-written metadata and a short phrase, and no licence is claimed for
  it. It stays because the deleted entries and their long-name runs in front
  of them are input a reader has to skip.


